    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d297">

                <pb facs="0295" n="283" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg283"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/8063: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1941-02-01T15:00:00+03:00">February 1, 1941—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 1—12:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>202. The Turkish Ambassador told me last night that in recent conversations with
                    Soviet officials they had intimated to him that in the event of a German
                    occupation of Bulgaria and Greece the Soviet attitude would be “the same as in
                    the case of the German occupation of Rumania”.</p>
                <p>The Ambassador expressed the opinion that the Turks would not enter Thrace in the
                    event of a German occupation but would defend themselves if attacked.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
